      ":question/worked-solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186096652/plots/baf8863e5158eddcae55cfffd1e0f9ec7961d80c63734aa00cd506acadaf6d34.png)\n\nIn $\u25b3OPR$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OP=4|p|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ and $p$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=4p.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $PR=4|q|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ and $q$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PR}=4q.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\  & =4p+4q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q=4p+4q.$\n\nSince $p\u2226q,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =4$ and $\\mu =4$",
